# ELM Migration — Command & Teams

Adapted from the Over Watch command structure. Same conventions, our names, our repo.

## Command

**Command is collective.** The three leads — Nolan, Jarrett, Thomas — hold it jointly. There is
no single authority above them; where the source structure had a Captain, we have consensus.

> Chain of command: **the three leads, collectively → each lead → their agents.**

That works only if "collective" means something concrete, so:

- **Anything outward-facing or hard to reverse needs a second lead's explicit sign-off** before it
  happens — merges to `origin/main`, anything pushed to `upstream`, dependency additions, package
  publishes, destructive operations. Get it in writing (a PR review, or a note in their `Notes/`).
  Tell the third lead after.
- **Anything reversible and inside your own scope is yours** — you do not need permission to work.
- **A disagreement between two leads goes to the third**, who breaks the tie. Whoever loses the
  call writes down what was decided and why, in the relevant ADR.
- **Nobody claims collective agreement they did not get.** "We discussed it" is not sign-off.

Each lead is accountable for what their agents do — an agent's mistake is that lead's to catch and
correct.

The migration is built by **three teams**, one per intern, each self-contained under
`Claude-Context/<Name>-Agents/` with its own **charters**, **notes inbox**, and **team syncs**.
This page is the map: who the teams are and how they talk to each other.

