## How a team is structured (the convention)

```
Claude-Context/<Name>-Agents/
  README.md                       team model + roster + scopes
  <AGENT>.md                      one charter per agent (use ../CHARTER-TEMPLATE.md)
  BACKLOG.md                      team claim board (rollup; lead-maintained) — SOURCE OF TRUTH
  BACKLOG-<AGENT>.md              (optional) per-agent detail view; IDs match the rollup
  Notes/                          async inbox (NOTE-<from-lead>-to-<to-lead>-YYYY-MM-DD-slug.md)
  syncs/                          team syncs (use ../SYNC-TEMPLATE.md)
```

New agent → follow [`NEW-AGENT.md`](NEW-AGENT.md), which walks the setup interactively and
produces the charter from [`CHARTER-TEMPLATE.md`](CHARTER-TEMPLATE.md).

Shared across all three teams, at `Claude-Context/` root:

```
claude-context-instruction        the entry point — every agent reads this first
Command-Structure                 this file
NEW-AGENT.md                      the agent onboarding prompt
GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md                fork, branches, sync cadence, concurrent work
OWNERSHIP.md                      path ownership + naming conventions
IN-FLIGHT.md                      cross-team claim board + rolling sync
ADR/                              decisions (why)
IMPL/                             implementation plans (how)
```

## Backlogs (the convention)

Every team keeps its **open work** in a backlog under `Claude-Context/<Name>-Agents/`, so all
three leads can see and **claim** work in one place — and so we're not all over the place.

- **`BACKLOG.md` — the team's canonical claim board (rollup).** One row per open item,
  lead-maintained. **This is the single source of truth for status + claims. Always claim here.**
- **`BACKLOG-<AGENT>.md` (optional) — an agent's detail/working view.** IDs match the rollup;
  it elaborates but must never contradict the rollup on status or claim.

**Row format (same for every team):**
`| ID | Item | Related ADR / IMPL | Status | Claimed by |`

- **ID** — `<TEAM>-<AGENT><n>`: team prefix + agent initial + number. Prefixes are **TN** (Nolan),
  **TJ** (Jarrett), **TT** (Thomas). So `TN-A1` = Team Nolan / agent whose name starts with A,
  item 1.
- **Status** — `PENDING` · `IN-PROGRESS` · `BLOCKED (<blocker id>)` · `ONGOING` · `DONE`.
- **Related docs** — link the ADR(s) and IMPL(s) behind the item.

**Claim protocol — git is the lock:**
- **To claim:** set `Claimed by` to your name + `Status → IN-PROGRESS`, and commit. One claimant
  per item; **first commit wins** (if you collide on rebase, pick another).
- **Another lead claims your team's item** → they work it directly and loop the Owner in via that
  team's `Notes/`.
- **On done:** `Status → DONE`, note the PR/commit; it drops off the next revision.

Backlogs hold **open work only** — shipped/merged work is not listed.

## Communication

**Within a team** — notes into your own team's `Notes/` inbox and periodic team syncs. Read your
inbox at the start of every session.

**Across teams** — drop a note in the **other team's** `Notes/` folder
(`Claude-Context/<OtherName>-Agents/Notes/`); that lead routes it to their agents. When two or
more teams need to align, hold a **cross-team sync** and write it up in the coordinating lead's
`syncs/` directory; all three leads attend.

**Note filename convention:** `NOTE-<from-lead>-to-<to-lead>-YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`
e.g. `NOTE-jarrett-to-nolan-2026-08-05-need-elm-provider-config-shape.md`

**`<from-lead>` and `<to-lead>` are always intern names — `nolan`, `jarrett`, `thomas` — never an
agent name.** A note drafted by an agent is still *from that agent's lead*, because that is how it
routes: you drop it in the other team's inbox, and **that lead routes it to their agents**. Three
reasons this matters:

- **The receiving team does not know your agents.** `NOTE-fluff-to-jarrett-…` tells Jarrett nothing
  about who is asking or with what authority; `NOTE-nolan-to-jarrett-…` tells them it came from a
  lead's team and who to reply to.
- **Agents are retired; leads are not.** An agent name in a filename goes stale the moment that
  agent is retired, and the filename is permanent — resolved notes are never deleted.
- **Replies have somewhere to go.** `NOTE-jarrett-to-nolan-…` is an obvious answer to
  `NOTE-nolan-to-jarrett-…`. Agent-addressed names do not pair up.

**Name the drafting agent in the note body, not the filename** — a `**Drafted by:**` line under the
title. Attribution is preserved; routing stays lead-to-lead.

**Within-team notes** (one agent to another on the same team) use
`NOTE-<lead>-internal-YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`, since `nolan-to-nolan` reads as a mistake.

A note is the right tool when: you need something another team owns, you changed something that
affects them, you found a defect in their territory, or you are about to touch a shared file.

**Notes are delivered by merging, not by writing.** A note exists only on the branch it was
committed to. Dropping a file in another team's `Notes/` on your own branch does not put it in
front of them — it reaches them when your work merges `<TeamBranch>` → `dev` and they merge `dev`
into theirs. Until then it is written, not sent. (Found 2026-08-11: six Team Nolan notes were
invisible to both other teams for this reason.)

**Optional inbox reader:** [`hooks/unread-notes.sh`](hooks/unread-notes.sh) prints your unread
notes into session context at session start. It is an artifact only — **nothing in this repo
executes it, and the wiring must never be committed.** This is a public fork of a public repo, so a
hook in `.claude/settings.json` would run on the machine of anyone who clones it. Wire it up in the
gitignored `.claude/settings.local.json` instead. See [`hooks/README.md`](hooks/README.md).

**Working at the same time as someone else** — notes are async and git is the lock, but neither
tells you what another agent is doing *right now*. See
[`GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md`](GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md) § *Two people working at once*.

## Doctrine — nothing broken leaves the shop

**A defect you find never goes unrecorded.** What you do about it depends on where you are.

**The default — you are mid-task: file a bug report and keep going.** Do not derail the work you
are on. Write the report properly (reproduction, `file:line`, what you verified and how), link it
from wherever you were working, and carry on. **A defect that is only in your head, or only in a
chat message, does not exist.**

**Fix it now if either is true:**

- **You have no other work in flight** — then it is your next task, not someone's someday.
- **It is an easy fix** — small, contained, low blast radius, and you can test it. Do not file a
  ticket for a one-line fix you could land in the time it takes to write the ticket. Still write
  the bug report; a fix with no record is how the same defect gets re-found in three months.

**Whichever path — the fix has to be real:**

1. **Verify it yourself first.** Reproduce it by execution, or read the code at `origin/main` and
   cite `file:line`. **A subagent's report is a lead, not a finding.** Confident claims resting on
   invented identifiers and stale line numbers cost more than the bug did.
2. **Fix the mechanism, not the symptom.** A fix that works for the wrong stated reason gets that
   reason quoted into the next three documents while the real defect survives.
3. **Write a test that FAILS on the old code, and prove that it fails.** Revert the fix, watch the
   test go red, restore it. **A green test nobody has seen go red is indistinguishable from no
   test.**
4. **Say what you did NOT fix, and why.** A partial fix presented as complete is worse than an
   honest gap, because it closes the ticket.

**Where it stops — these are the line, not loopholes:**

- **Outward-facing or hard to reverse needs a second lead's sign-off** — merges to `origin/main`,
  anything reaching `upstream`, dependency additions, anything destructive. Find it, write the
  fix, then get the go-ahead. This doctrine authorises *fixing*; it never authorises *shipping
  unasked*.
- **Crossing another team's seam:** if you fix it, drop a note in the owning team's `Notes/` the
  same session. Surgical, tested, and flagged where it changes their behaviour. **Fix and tell —
  never fix and hide.** If you would rather not cross the seam, file the report and route it to
  their lead.
- **If the fix turns out bigger than the finding**, stop and write it up rather than half-doing it.

**The honesty half — it applies to your own work too.** When you find that something you already
published was wrong, correct it **everywhere it landed**, not only in the newest document.
Corrections that live only in the latest artifact are how a wrong number gets quoted for a week.
Retract in place, in the original, where the reader actually is.

### The ELM-specific corollary

An ELM that scores badly on a call site is a **finding, not a failure** — record it in an ADR with
the numbers. But the reverse is the trap this project will actually hit:

**Never report an accuracy number without its seed and its baseline.** Three classes at 40%
accuracy is worse than guessing. "It works" is not a result, and a demo that ran once on your
machine is not evidence. If it isn't a committed, seeded script another team can run, it didn't
happen.

## Rules that bind every team

- **Never commit to `main`.** Branch as `elm/<name>/<short-topic>`. Merging to `origin/main`
  needs a second lead's sign-off. Full workflow: [`GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md`](GITHUB-WORKFLOW.md).
- **Own your seam.** Once scopes are assigned, work that drifts into another team's scope gets a
  note and a hand-off — never a quiet reach across the boundary. See `OWNERSHIP.md`.
- **One agent, one worktree.** Two agents must never share a working directory. See below.
- **The ELM is a registered vendor, not a fork.** `ProviderRegistry.register(vendor, factory)`
  (`packages/llm-client/src/provider-registry.ts:96`) is the extension seam. Bolting ELM into the
  existing provider files guarantees three-way conflicts — all three teams have reason to touch
  them.
- **Architecture tests are not advisory.** The tier hierarchy and gateway rules are enforced by
  `tests/e2e/architecture-policy.test.js` and `tests/e2e/domain-isolation.test.js`. Run
  `pnpm typecheck && pnpm test` before you claim anything is done.
- **Announce dependency additions before running `pnpm add`.** `pnpm-lock.yaml` conflicts
  brutally. Claim it in `IN-FLIGHT.md`. Resolve lockfile conflicts by re-running `pnpm install`,
  never by hand-merging.
- **Changeset bumps default to `patch`** unless the leads agree otherwise (root `CLAUDE.md`).

