## One agent, one worktree

**Every agent that may work concurrently with another gets its own git worktree.** Not a
convention to observe when convenient — the shared checkout is how an agent's uncommitted work
gets destroyed, and it is not recoverable.

```bash
git worktree add ../n-dx-<name> -b elm/<name>/<topic>
```

Declare the path in your team's roster **Worktree** column. `(TBD)` and `(shared checkout)` are
not valid entries for an agent that works alongside others.

A fresh worktree has **no `node_modules`**; run `pnpm install` before tests. Don't symlink or share
one between worktrees — branches can carry different lockfiles, and a silently-shared
`node_modules` produces wrong-dependency bugs much harder to diagnose than a missing one.

### The n-dx-specific reason this is not optional

Beyond the general shared-checkout hazard, this repo has a second one that is worse because it is
silent. The root `CLAUDE.md` documents it: `.rex/prd_tree/`, `.sourcevision/`, and `.hench/` have
**no file locking**. Concurrent writers lose data with **no error** — the last writer simply wins.

Any of `ndx plan`, `ndx work`, `ndx ci`, `ndx refresh`, `ndx self-heal`, or a rex MCP write tool
run from a shared checkout while another agent is doing the same will corrupt PRD state, and you
will not find out for days. A worktree gives each agent its own `.rex/` and `.sourcevision/`, which
makes the whole class of problem disappear.

If you are on a shared checkout anyway: **claim it in `IN-FLIGHT.md` before running any of those
commands, and release it after.** Read-only (`ndx status`, `ndx usage`) is always safe.

### The rules

1. **Never `git stash` in a shared checkout.** A worktree makes stashing unnecessary, so this rule
   costs nothing. Suppressing stderr on a stash/pop pair (`2>/dev/null`) turns a failure into a
   silent loss; don't.
2. **Check `git branch --show-current` before you edit**, not before you commit. By commit time
   the damage is already staged.
3. **Commit early and in small steps**, including WIP safety commits. This is the practice that
   saves work when something else goes wrong, and it is cheap.
4. **Never `git add -A`** in a checkout another agent may be using — you will commit their work
   under your name and message. Stage explicit paths.
5. **Prefer a throwaway worktree even when you are alone.**

